// The position is NOT an address. Adding a key changes what sits at each
// position, so a program that depended on d[2] would stop being correct
// with nothing to say so. This is Python's position on dict.
d = #(a: 1, b: 2)

!? {
    >> d[2] ¶
} :! {
    >> "refused: a dictionary is addressed by key, not by position" ¶
}

// Whole family, not just the read — a positional WRITE would corrupt data
!? {
    d[2]$~ 99
} :! {
    >> "refused: d[n]$~ has no meaning here" ¶
}

// The positional tuple keeps the whole family: there the index is the
// only address there is, and the size is fixed.
t = (10, 20, 30)
>> t[2] ¶
